Basal Cell Carcinoma
Haematology & Oncology
Pearly translucent papule, Arborizing telangiectasias, Rolled border with central ulceration, Chronically sun-exposed location

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-006
Reason for Visit: Continuous, unremitting bleeding from a facial lesion following minor trauma requiring urgent haemostasis and wound assessment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute, persistent bleeding from a facial nodule that failed to arrest with 30 minutes of direct pressure by the patient. Urgent in-person evaluation was mandated to achieve definitive haemostasis, prevent clinically significant overnight blood loss, and assess for secondary bacterial infection, satisfying criteria for immediate after-hours intervention.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I scratched this bump on my forehead with a towel, and it has been dripping blood for two hours straight no matter how hard I press on it."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 6-month history of a slow-growing, painless bump on the right forehead. The lesion began as a small translucent spot and slowly expanded. This evening, while drying their face, the patient inadvertently traumatized the area. This provoked sudden, continuous bleeding that has soaked multiple tissues. The patient denies any recent systemic illness, fever, or prior episodes of severe bleeding from other sites.
Functional Status: Independent; highly anxious due to the visual impact of facial bleeding.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient lives alone; risk of blood-soiled linens creating a slip hazard.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, visibly anxious adult holding a blood-soaked towel to the right forehead.
Physical Exam:
Skin: A 1.5 cm pearly, dome-shaped papule is present on the right superior forehead (a chronically sun-exposed location). The lesion features raised, rolled borders with prominent arborizing telangiectasias traversing the surface. A central ulceration is actively oozing venous blood. No palpable regional cervical or preauricular lymphadenopathy.
Cardiovascular: Heart sounds dual, no murmurs, normal capillary refill centrally.
Neurological: Cranial nerves II-XII intact. No focal sensory or motor deficits.
Environmental Observations: Blood droplets noted on the bathroom floor, presenting a localized slip hazard.
Medication Reconciliation: No concurrent use of aspirin, clopidogrel, or oral anticoagulants verified.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Basal Cell Carcinoma - Status: Exacerbated (Actively Bleeding)
Differentials:

Squamous Cell Carcinoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the distinct pearly, translucent appearance and presence of arborizing telangiectasias, rather than a hyperkeratotic or scaly morphology.

Melanoma (Amelanotic) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the slow, relentless growth over months and characteristic rolled borders, lacking rapid morphologic changes or irregular pigmentation.

Seborrheic Keratosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the dome-shaped, telangiectatic presentation rather than a sharply demarcated, "stuck-on," waxy appearance.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an actively bleeding facial lesion that failed conservative patient-led management. The objective morphological features strongly suggest a Basal Cell Carcinoma with central ulceration. The friable nature of the tumour bed prevents normal primary haemostasis. Delaying medical assessment until the next in-hours period would subject the patient to ongoing haemorrhage, escalating psychological distress, and an increased risk of wound bed infection. This acute haemorrhagic complication of a chronic lesion definitively necessitates urgent after-hours intervention to achieve mechanical or chemical haemostasis and stabilize the patient.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Application of focal chemical cautery (silver nitrate) and a sterile pressure dressing achieved complete haemostasis. Urgent routine referral generated for outpatient dermatological excision and histopathology.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Leave the pressure dressing completely undisturbed for 24 hours. By preventing physical disruption of the new clot, you eliminate the trigger for recurrent bleeding.

Apply a broad-spectrum sunscreen daily to all exposed areas. By actively blocking ultraviolet radiation, you reduce the environmental damage that promotes further cellular mutations and new skin lesions.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days for wound check and definitive excision planning.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or call 000 if pulsatile bleeding restarts and cannot be stopped with 15 minutes of firm, continuous pressure, or if you develop sudden dizziness or lightheadedness.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The clinical appearance strongly suggests a specific type of skin cancer, but this requires a biopsy under a microscope for absolute confirmation.
Clinical Course: The bleeding has been stopped, but the underlying fragile tissue remains. It will likely bleed again if bumped before it is surgically removed.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not remove the dressing tonight. If bleeding completely soaks through the bandage and drips, apply firm pressure over the top and proceed to the ED.

PATIENT EDUCATION

